      "text": "To find the smallest number of coins that meets the given conditions, we can represent the number of coins as \\( N \\) and check the congruences:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by examining the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 3 - 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo, \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nThus, the general solution for \\( N \\) is:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nSo, the box contains 28 coins. Now, we need to determine how many coins are left when these 28 coins are equally divided among seven people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\]\nThus, when 28 coins are equally divided among seven people, there are:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]\ncoins left over.",
