      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Firstly, let \\( N \\) be the number of coins in the box. The conditions given are:\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 4. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 3. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Now substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplify the congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 6k + 4 - 1 + 1 \\equiv 4 + 1 - 1 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\implies 6k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\implies 6k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\( 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\), we have:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus, \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substitute this back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) must be of the form:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive \\( N \\) is when \\( m = 0 \\), thus:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\nNow, we need to divide 28 coins equally among 7 people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\]\nThere is no remainder, so the number of coins left when 28 are equally divided among 7 people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
